ap2 buffered audio: resync instead of skipping forever after a large backward timestamp jump

When an iOS 26 source changes track, it restarts the RTP timeline far behind
the expected timestamp without a flush. Every block then looks "too old",
is skipped, and because expected_timestamp only advances for blocks that are
played, every following block is skipped as well: the receiver stays silent
until a new session starts. Only treat a backward jump as a stale
retransmission when it is small (under five seconds); a larger one is a new
timeline, so play the block (muted, as after any discontinuity) and let
expected_timestamp follow it.

--- a/ap2_buffered_audio_processor.c
+++ b/ap2_buffered_audio_processor.c
@@ -636,7 +636,14 @@
                       // (?)
 
                       int32_t abs_timestamp_difference = -timestamp_difference;
-                      if ((size_t)abs_timestamp_difference > get_ssrc_block_length(payload_ssrc)) {
+                      // A jump of more than a few seconds backwards is a new timeline
+                      // (e.g. a track change on iOS 26), not a stale retransmission:
+                      // play it (muted) so expected_timestamp resyncs, instead of
+                      // skipping every block from now on.
+                      int32_t resync_threshold = 5 * (int32_t)conn->input_rate;
+                      if (((size_t)abs_timestamp_difference >
+                           get_ssrc_block_length(payload_ssrc)) &&
+                          (abs_timestamp_difference < resync_threshold)) {
                         skip_this_block = 1;
                         debug(2,
